+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `rustfft`
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2), added to
+`tests/accuracy.rs` (module `hegel_properties`), against upstream `ejmahler/RustFFT` at
+v6.4.1 (commit `4758ab0`). The crate's point is planning FFTs of *every* length (butterflies,
+Radix4, RadixN, mixed-radix, Good-Thomas, Rader's, Bluestein's), so the central generator is
+a widely-drawn length: all of 0–64, powers of two up to 8192, a prime table up to 10007
+(chosen to hit both Rader's and Bluestein's — e.g. 59 and 10007 force Bluestein because
+`len-1` has a prime factor > 23), smooth composites built by multiplying drawn small factors
+(generating the mixed-radix *configuration*, not just payloads), and arbitrary lengths up to
+4096. Signals are drawn with components in ±1e9 (finite; magnitude cap protects the test's
+error model, and tolerances scale with the same bound). Run on aarch64, so the scalar and
+NEON SIMD planner paths are the ones exercised (AVX/SSE compile to stubs here).
+
+All tolerances are explicit and calibrated as
+`32 · eps · (log2(n) + 8) · magnitude_bound`, with `magnitude_bound = n · max|x|` for an
+unnormalized transform (energy-squared units for Parseval), doubled per extra transform,
+and floored at `f64::MIN_POSITIVE` for subnormal-scale signals (see below).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Ten distinct properties, one hegel test each:
+
+1. **`hegel_forward_inverse_roundtrip_is_identity_scaled_by_len`** — the crown roundtrip:
+   unnormalized forward then inverse (order drawn) reproduces the input scaled by `n`, for
+   any length including 0 and 1. *Evidence:* the crate-level docs' normalization contract
+   ("callers can normalize once by scaling each element by `1/len()`").
+2. **`hegel_planned_fft_matches_naive_dft_definition`** — for lengths ≤ 256, the planned
+   FFT matches an independently transcribed O(n²) DFT-definition oracle
+   (`X[k] = Σ x[j]·e^(∓2πi·jk/n)`, computed with `std` trig, not the crate's twiddle code),
+   both directions. *Evidence:* the DFT definition itself; the crate's own `Dft` doc says
+   naive DFT is the reference for all other algorithms.
+3. **`hegel_fft_is_linear`** — `FFT(a·x + b·y) == a·FFT(x) + b·FFT(y)` with drawn complex
+   scalars, both directions. *Evidence:* linearity of the DFT sum.
+4. **`hegel_parseval_energy_conservation`** — `Σ|x[j]|² == (1/n)·Σ|X[k]|²` (relative-scale
+   tolerance in magnitude² units). *Evidence:* Parseval's theorem under the unnormalized
+   convention.
+5. **`hegel_constant_signal_transforms_to_dc_delta`** — a constant signal `c` transforms to
+   `X[0] = n·c` and `X[k≠0] ≈ 0`, both directions. The zero bins are a cancellation stress
+   test.
+6. **`hegel_circular_time_shift_multiplies_spectrum_by_phase`** — shift theorem: circular
+   left-shift by `s` multiplies bin `k` by `e^(±2πi·sk/n)` (sign per direction), with the
+   phase factors computed from `std` trig independently of crate twiddles.
+7. **`hegel_all_process_entry_points_agree`** — `process_with_scratch`,
+   `process_outofplace_with_scratch`, and `process_immutable_with_scratch` compute the same
+   transform within single-transform tolerance. *Evidence:* the `Fft` trait docs describe
+   all three as computing the same FFT.
+8. **`hegel_multi_chunk_buffer_equals_chunkwise_processing`** — a buffer of `k·n` elements
+   processed in one call bit-exactly equals processing each chunk separately (tolerance 0:
+   documented contract "divides `buffer` into chunks of size `self.len()`, and computes a
+   FFT on each chunk", same deterministic computation either way).
+9. **`hegel_planner_is_deterministic`** — planning the same `(len, direction)` in two fresh
+   planners yields the same scratch length and bit-identical output for the same input
+   (tolerance 0).
+10. **`hegel_f32_and_f64_planners_agree`** — the `f32` and `f64` planners agree on the same
+    f32-representable signal within an f32-eps-scaled tolerance (the f64 result acts as the
+    reference; the two planners choose independent SIMD/scalar implementations per type).
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full suite green: `cargo test` → 122 lib unit tests, 14 `tests/accuracy.rs` tests
+  (4 pre-existing + 10 hegel), 17 doctests — 0 failures.
+- **Liveness check performed:** temporarily dropping the `· n` scaling from the roundtrip
+  expectation produced a shrunk minimal counterexample
+  (`n = 2, signal = [0, i]`, error `1e0` vs tolerance `2.6e-13`), then reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10× run performed** (`test_cases = 1000` on all ten tests): all green in
+  ~7 s; drawn lengths observed (via temporary instrumentation) to span 0 through 5103,
+  covering primes (59, 131, 251, 307, 839, 2003), powers of two, and smooth composites
+  (144, 288, 2475, 3024, 5103). Committed tests use the default case count.
+- No `.hegel/` failure-database residue committed; `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None. All ten properties hold at default and 10× case counts, across scalar and NEON code
+paths, all planner algorithm families, lengths 0–10007, and signals spanning ~19 orders of
+magnitude including zero, subnormals, and mixed scales. The one failure seen during
+development was a defect in the *test's* tolerance model, not the library (next section).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Eps-relative tolerance without a subnormal floor.** The first version of
+  `fft_tolerance` (`32·eps·(log2 n + 8)·n·max|x|`) underflowed to `0.0` when hegel drew a
+  subnormal constant signal (`c = 1.02e-312`, `n = 11`): the FFT's off-DC bins came back as
+  one denormal ULP (`5e-324` — the smallest positive f64) instead of exactly 0, which any
+  floating-point FFT legitimately does at subnormal scale (twiddle products round with
+  absolute error up to one denormal ULP). Fixed by flooring the tolerance at
+  `f64::MIN_POSITIVE` (~2.2e-308) — far above accumulated denormal rounding, astronomically
+  below any real bug — with the rationale documented in the code. The f32-vs-f64 tolerance
+  got the analogous `f32::MIN_POSITIVE` floor.
+- **"`process_immutable_with_scratch` leaves the input untouched"** was considered and
+  dropped as a tautology: the input is `&[Complex<T>]`, so Rust's type system already
+  enforces it — nothing for a property to falsify.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Oracle Sourcing section's "spec, transcribed" pattern fit perfectly (DFT definition
+  with independent `std` trig as the oracle for small n), and "generate configurations, not
+  just payloads" mapped directly onto generating the planner's *length* (the factor
+  structure is the configuration). Both were the right prompts.
+- The floating-point guidance ("calibrate tolerance to n · eps · max_magnitude") worked but
+  has a hole the skill could name: **a purely multiplicative eps-based tolerance underflows
+  to zero on subnormal-scale inputs**, producing a false failure of one denormal ULP.
+  A one-line note — "floor absolute tolerances at `MIN_POSITIVE` (with a comment) so
+  subnormal-scale draws don't produce spurious 1-ULP failures" — would have saved the one
+  debugging round this exercise needed. It's the absolute-tolerance sibling of the existing
+  "audit a floating-point oracle before trusting a tiny disagreement" note.
+- Rust reference accuracy: `--ignore-rust-version` for MSRV 1.61 was needed exactly as the
+  Setup section predicts; `one_of!` over heterogeneous generator types needed `.boxed()` on
+  every branch exactly as documented; `#[hegel::composite]` with a runtime `usize` parameter
+  (exact-size signal vectors) worked first try.
